https://issues.jenkins.io/browse/JENKINS-75563
Implements the windows side of 


      kubernetes-plugin/src/main/java/org/csanchez/jenkins/plugins/kubernetes/pipeline/ContainerExecDecorator.java


         Line 661
      in
      5054f32







           public void kill(Map<String, String> modelEnvVars) throws IOException, InterruptedException { 




 as best as I can, a powershell script finds processes withg the JENKINS_SERVER_COOKIE and then it tries to kill them by using some powershell magic to:

Sending ctrl-c
Stop-Process -Force=false
Stop-Process

Unlike in unix systems windows signal handling is not so easy so this is a best effort in the best case, for example ctrl-c will not be able to gracefully kill a powershell script launched by the powershell step (see #1724 (comment) for details, or batch scripts which will always ask for user confirmation and do not have any ability to react to ctrl-c. But at least this kills the processes which is more than what we had before so files are unblocked in case of need, for example this pipeline.
  agent {
    kubernetes {
      customWorkspace 'c:/s'
      yaml """
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
spec:
  containers:
  - name: jnlp
    image: "cloudbees/cloudbees-core-agent:latest-jdk21-windowsservercore-ltsc2022"
    imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent
    volumeMounts:
    - mountPath: /s
      name: s-volume
    - mountPath: /s@tmp
      name: stmp-volume
    resources:
      cpu:
        request: 1
      memory:
        request: 2Gi
  volumes:
  - emptyDir: {}
    name: s-volume
  - emptyDir: {}
    name: stmp-volume
  nodeSelector:
    kubernetes.io/os: windows
    node.kubernetes.io/windows-build: 10.0.20348
"""
    }
  }

  stages {
    stage('Timeout'){
        options {
        timeout(time: 10, unit: "SECONDS")
    }
        steps{
            container('jnlp') {
                script{
                    try {
                        bat 'ping 127.0.0.1 -n 3601 > test.txt'
                    } catch(Exception ex) {
                        bat 'rename test.txt test2.txt' 
                    } 
                }
            }
        }
    }       
  }
}

This was failing before because the attempt to kill process failed and hence test.txt was blocked by the ping command, with my change it finish successfully. It is arguably not a good practice for pipeline development, but given the blocking nature of windows I believe this is better than the nothing we had before.
