Summary
Fixes the inheritance bug reported by @eliasbalasis on #20: remoteFs (and image, numExecutors, maxInstances, mode) was not inherited from a parent template, so a child always ran in the default /home/jenkins/agent working directory with no way to override it through inheritance.
Root cause
The constructor pre-filled five fields with defaults:
this.image = "jenkins/inbound-agent:latest";
this.remoteFs = "/home/jenkins/agent";
this.numExecutors = 1;
this.maxInstances = 5;
this.mode = Node.Mode.NORMAL;
resolve() inherits with this.x != null/> 0 ? this.x : parent. Because the constructor made those fields always non-null/positive, the this.x branch always won and the parent value was never used — these five fields could never be inherited. Fields with no constructor default (cpuLimit, memoryLimit, entrypoint, …) inherit correctly, which is why the PR #20 tests didn't catch it.
Fix
Leave the five fields unset in the constructor. Their getters already supply the defaults (getRemoteFs() → /home/jenkins/agent, etc.), so external behaviour is unchanged, but resolve() now sees an unset (null/0) value for a field the user didn't configure and inherits it from the parent. The resolve() logic itself was already null/0-aware and is untouched.
getAvailableCapacity() read the maxInstances field directly (would now be 0 for a default template), so it's routed through getMaxInstances().
Testing
Written test-first. New cases pin both directions:

a child that sets none of the five fields inherits the parent's remoteFs, image, numExecutors, maxInstances and mode;
an explicit child value still overrides the parent.

Full suite green: 351 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors (6 pre-existing skips) — including the CasC export and provisioning tests, confirming the getter-supplied defaults keep external behaviour intact.
Relates to #20.

Summary
Fixes the "storm of unused nodes" reported by @eliasbalasis on #20: templates with no labelString (typically base templates kept only for inheritance) provision a large number of agents that are never used.
This is not specific to multi-level inheritance — #20 only touched resolve(). The inheritance feature merely makes a latent bug visible, because it's now natural to keep label-less "base" templates.
Root cause
SwarmAgentTemplate.matches() treats a template with no labelString as a catch-all:
if (!isNotBlank(labelString)) {
    return mode == Node.Mode.NORMAL;   // matches ANY labelled job
}
So a label-less base template matches every NORMAL-mode job. getTemplate() returns the first match, so it picks the base template and provisions an agent that does not carry the requested label. The queued job can't run on that agent, Jenkins re-asks, another unused base node is provisioned — and the cycle repeats, bounded only by maxInstances / maxConcurrentAgents. This also contradicts Jenkins core semantics: a NORMAL node with no labels should only serve label-less jobs, not jobs that require a specific label.
Fix
matches() now evaluates the resolved template, so:

a template with no effective label (its own or inherited) only matches label-less jobs, instead of hijacking labelled ones;
labels inherited through inheritFrom are honoured, keeping template selection consistent with the resolved configuration that is actually provisioned.

public boolean matches(@Nullable Label label) {
    SwarmAgentTemplate resolved = resolve();
    if (label == null) {
        return resolved.getMode() == Node.Mode.NORMAL;
    }
    if (!isNotBlank(resolved.getLabelString())) {
        return false;
    }
    return label.matches(resolved.getLabelSet());
}
Tests
Added regression coverage (written test-first):

matchesRejectsLabelledJobWhenTemplateHasNoLabel — a label-less template must not match a labelled job;
matchesUsesInheritedLabels — a leaf with no own label matches a label inherited from its parent;
getTemplateSkipsUnlabelledBaseTemplateForLabelledJob — getTemplate() picks the labelled leaf, and does not fall through to the base template for unknown labels.

Full suite green: 341 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors (6 pre-existing skips).

