IaC scanning previously worked only as a Freestyle build step. The only way to run it in a Pipeline was calling the internal IaCScanningBuilder class directly (step([$class: 'IaCScanningBuilder', ...])), an unsupported contract with no public parameters that breaks silently when internals change.
This adds a first-class sysdigIaCScan Pipeline step (declarative and scripted) with a stable, backwards-compatible parameter set, mirroring how VM scanning exposes sysdigImageScan. Freestyle behaviour is unchanged.
New Pipeline syntax
Minimal (only engineCredentialsId is mandatory):
pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Sysdig IaC Scan') {
            steps {
                sysdigIaCScan engineCredentialsId: 'sysdig-secure-api-credentials'
            }
        }
    }
}
All options are exposed as named, backwards-compatible parameters:
sysdigIaCScan engineCredentialsId: 'sysdig-secure-api-credentials',
              path: 'infrastructure/',
              isRecursive: true,
              listUnsupported: false,
              severityThreshold: 'high',
              sysdigEnv: 'https://secure.sysdig.com',
              version: 'latest'
Reporting interface
The scan results are now visible instead of log-only. The builder requests the scanner's JSON report (--output-json), parses it into a domain model, and logs a per-severity summary. Each build gets a Sysdig Secure IaC Report page with summary tiles and a sortable, severity-filterable findings table, plus the unsupported resources and parse errors reported by the scanner.

The build action persists only the raw scanner JSON and re-parses it lazily on render, so it round-trips cleanly through the build's storage without relying on record serialization. The scanner exit code remains the source of truth for pass/fail. README updated with pipeline examples, a parameter table, and screenshots.

