Until now no CI job upgraded a controller. Every shard starts from a fresh JENKINS_HOME built with the current hpi. Now that a release exists (1414.vc347803c1997), a change that breaks loading existing stores would reach users unseen. That includes XStream field renames, class moves and id formats (SPEC 4 restart durability, SPEC 6a "an upgrade never stops a schedule", D-68).
This adds the unit h3-upgrade (e2e/h3/upgrade.py) to the group "job ui params and restart".
Its own controller. The unit starts a second controller next to the shard's, using e2e/h3/compose.yml. It has its own compose project, volume and port, and the same image. It never touches the shard's Jenkins and removes itself at the end. run.sh also removes it if the step is killed.
Phase A, the released plugin.

The released hpi is downloaded from updates.jenkins.io, with retries on 429 and 5xx. It is checked against a pinned sha256, 9fabaa19…3706, which matches the update center's plugin-versions.json.
The data is made mostly as users make it:

the Batch Control strategy;
the configuration, saved in the browser;
run requests in all five statuses, one with an approved run waiting in the queue;
an approved CONFIGURE window and a pending DELETE window request;
an activated every-minute cron job, and one whose activation is still pending;
an acknowledged incident;
a record from last month, so there are two month buckets.


A snapshot records every entity's getters through the store, plus pages, notices, CSV exports, config, strategy, monitors, store files and queue.

Phase B. The controller is stopped cleanly, then re-created on the same volume with target/batch-control.hpi.
Phase C, 45 PASS/FAIL h3 <check> lines.

Loads cleanly:

the new version is active;
the new boot's log has no XStream or class errors and no plugin WARNING or SEVERE line;
there is no old-data entry, and the monitors are unchanged.


Same state:

strategy and all 12 settings;
every entity, with every getter equal;
page statuses, notices and CSV rows;
store files and the restored queue;
history listings, with both month buckets.
The one value a restart legitimately moves is the approved request's expiry base (SPEC 7). It is checked to move later, never earlier.


Still works:

the queued approved run starts exactly once with its parameters;
the activated cron job's timer fires after the upgrade (SPEC 6a), and the held one still does not;
the pending run request can be approved and runs;
the window still confers its permission, and the other item still refuses it;
pending window and activation requests can be decided, a new request can be filed, and the incident can be resolved.



Red/green, locally.

The unit was green 3 times, once of them through ci/run.sh.
With BC_H3_TAMPER changing the store between the phases, the matching checks went red:

a corrupted run request file;
a deleted grant;
a renamed config field (approvers → approverIds);
a deleted activation state, which shows the SPEC 6a check is not vacuous.



Workflow. The e2e job caches e2e/ci/.cache/released (actions/cache v6.1.0, pinned by SHA). The digest is checked whether the file came from the cache or a download.
Cost. The unit adds about 100 to 130 s, mostly two boots and two cron minutes; its weight is 2.5. Its group becomes the heaviest, at an estimated 29 minutes against the 90-minute job timeout.
Note. src/main has not changed since the release, so today this upgrades the same code under a new version. It guards the next change.
I ran the full E2E 3 times on the fork (41201323346, 41201328740, 41201333989). All 3 were green, each with all 45 h3 checks passing and 0 FAIL lines.
Not covered:

upgrades from 1124 and 1244;
Pipeline, folder and multibranch jobs;
file and secret parameters (r19/restart.py covers those across a restart);
role-strategy, JCasC-set configuration, retention and mail.

